+# CIS-2 section 14, seeded sampling (v0.4 DRAFT, informative until adopted)
+
+Status: draft text for the next specification version. v0.3b is greedy-only
+and unchanged; nothing here moves a pinned digest. Every value below is a
+CANDIDATE produced by the lab's C++ port (host and CUDA builds) and
+re-derived by a from-text numpy implementation written in the same lab; no
+clean-room implementation by another party has run it yet.
+
+Why: no public specification we could find pins a pseudo-random source, its
+seeding and the uniform-to-float conversion so that two independent
+implementations draw the same token sequence bit for bit. Engines that offer
+seeded reproducibility offer it on one engine and one hardware class. CIS-2
+already pins every arithmetic step of the forward pass, the exponential
+(section 6.2), the logarithm (section 6.5) and the first-index argmax
+(section 11.2). A stateless sampler therefore needs one new primitive: a
+pinned 32-bit hash. Everything else reuses pinned operations.
+
+## 14.0 Scope
+
+With `sample_mode = 1` the decode step picks the next token by Gumbel-max
+sampling over the fp32 logits of section 11 with a stateless, seed-keyed
+noise source. `sample_mode = 0` is the greedy decode of section 11.2; its
+receipts are byte-identical to v0.3b.
+
+## 14.1 Parameters (all part of the receipt header, 14.5)
+
+- `seed`: u64.
+- `temperature`: f32, finite and `> 0`. `temperature == 0` with
+  `sample_mode = 1` is INVALID; use `sample_mode = 0`.
+- `top_k`: u32; `0` = disabled; a value `>= V` is treated as disabled.
+- `top_p`: f32 in `(0, 1]`; `1.0` = disabled.
+
+An implementation that receives an invalid parameter rejects the run. A
+verifier that receives a receipt whose header carries an invalid parameter
+reports the receipt as INVALID rather than re-running it.
+
+When parameters are given as decimal text, they are converted to f32 by
+correctly rounded decimal-to-binary conversion (as `strtof` does): `"0.8"`
+is `0x3f4ccccd`, `"0.95"` is `0x3f733333`. The receipt binds the f32 bit
+patterns, not the text.
+
+## 14.2 Noise source (the one new primitive)
+
+- Hash: `MurmurHash3_x86_32` (Appleby, public domain; the function of that
+  name in the reference `MurmurHash3.cpp`), with hash seed parameter
+  `0x43495332` (ASCII `CIS2`), over the 16-byte key
+  `seed (u64 LE, 8 bytes) || step (u32 LE, 4 bytes) || vocab_index (u32 LE, 4 bytes)`.
+  `step` is the 0-based generation step (the loop variable of the section 11
+  decode: the first generated token is step 0). The hash is computed for the
+  key length 16 (four 4-byte blocks, empty tail); the finalisation mixes the
+  length as the reference does. Known answers: the empty key with hash seed
+  0 gives `00000000` and with hash seed 1 gives `514e28b7` (the published
+  reference values), and the four rows of 14.6.
+- Uniform: `m = (h >> 9) * 2 + 1`, `u = f32(m) * 2^-24`. `m` is odd and
+  below `2^24`, so `f32(m)` is exact and `u` is exact, in
+  `(2^-24, 1 - 2^-24)`; `ln_pinned(u)` is finite and negative.
+- Gumbel: `g = 0.0f - ln_pinned(0.0f - ln_pinned(u))`: two section 6.5
+  calls and two exact negations, no fused multiply-add, no other arithmetic.
+  The inner argument is positive, so no domain branch of section 6.5 is
+  reached; for `u` near 1 the inner logarithm is a small negative normal
+  number (magnitude above `2^-25`).
+
+The noise for `(seed, step, index)` is a pure function. It does not depend
+on the batch, the order of evaluation, the vocabulary size, the hardware or
+any generator state.
+
+## 14.3 Candidate set, computed on the section 11 logits `l[0..V)`
+
+1. `s_i = l_i / temperature` (f32 division, always performed; division by
+   `1.0f` is exact).
+2. Rank order: `i` precedes `j` iff `s_i > s_j`, or `s_i == s_j` and
+   `i < j`. `rank(i)` is the number of indices that precede `i`.
+3. top-k: if `top_k > 0` (and `top_k < V`), keep `i` iff `rank(i) < top_k`.
+4. top-p: if `top_p < 1.0`, over the indices kept by step 3 (all `V` when
+   top-k is disabled), in rank order: `m = s` of rank 0;
+   `e_r = exp_pinned(s_r - m)` (section 6.2, f32 subtraction);
+   `denom = sum_seq(e)` in rank order (section 5.3 sequential f32 sum);
+   `p_r = e_r / denom` (plain f32 division);
+   `c_r = sum_seq(p_0 .. p_r)` in rank order (the running f32 sum, so
+   `c_r = c_{r-1} + p_r`); keep rank `r` iff `r == 0` or `c_{r-1} < top_p`.
+   `c` is non-decreasing, so the kept ranks are a prefix: the first rank
+   that fails ends it.
+
+Any algorithm that yields the same kept set is acceptable; sorting is not
+required.
+
+## 14.4 Draw
+
+For kept `i`: `z_i = s_i + g_i` (f32 addition). For masked `i`:
+`z_i = -inf`. `next_id` is the FIRST index attaining the maximum of `z`
+(the strict-greater scan of section 11.2 starting at index 0, applied to
+`z`). Rank 0 is always kept, so the maximum is finite.
+
+## 14.5 Receipt
+
+Section 12.1 is unchanged for `sample_mode = 0`. For `sample_mode = 1`,
+immediately after the prompt token ids (section 12.1 item 6) and before the
+step-0 logits, the witness chain absorbs, in this order: the ASCII bytes
+`CIS2-SAMPLE-1` (13 bytes), `seed` (u64 LE, 8 bytes), `temperature` (f32
+bit pattern as u32 LE, 4 bytes), `top_k` (u32 LE, 4 bytes), `top_p` (f32
+bit pattern as u32 LE, 4 bytes). Item 7 is unchanged: per step the full fp32
+logit vector (7a) then `next_id` (7b, now the sampled id). The argmax digest
+of section 12.2 keeps its name and is fed the sampled ids. A greedy verifier
+re-running a sampled receipt with `sample_mode = 0` diverges from step 1 on;
+that is the intended behaviour, not a defect.
+
+## 14.6 Known-answer vectors (CANDIDATE)
+
+Hash seed `0x43495332`, key as in 14.2. `u_bits` and `g_bits` are the f32
+bit patterns.
+
+| seed | step | index | hash | u_bits | g_bits |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `0x0000000000000001` | 0 | 0 | `07731ea2` | `3cee63e0` | `bfa1b32b` |
+| `0x0000000000000001` | 0 | 1 | `c832451b` | `3f483245` | `3fb392c7` |
+| `0x0000000000000001` | 1 | 0 | `c0d0c4fa` | `3f40d0c5` | `3fa15ffe` |
+| `0xffffffffffffffff` | 7 | 49151 | `e682298d` | `3f668229` | `40104f5a` |
+
+Toy vector: `V = 8`, `l = [1.0, 2.0, 2.0, -1.0, 0.5, 3.0, 3.0, -4.0]`,
+`seed = 1`, `step = 0`, `temperature = 0.8` (`0x3f4ccccd`), `top_k = 4`,
+`top_p = 0.9` (`0x3f666666`). Kept set `{1, 2, 5, 6}` (top-k keeps ranks 0
+to 3, which are indices 5, 6, 1, 2; the running sums before ranks 1, 2 and 3
+are below 0.9, so top-p keeps all four). `z` bit patterns for the kept
+indices: `z_1 = 4079c964`, `z_2 = 4026f5c5`, `z_5 = 407320fd`,
+`z_6 = 4011c83a`; every masked `z` is `ff800000`. `next_id = 1`.
+
+Model vector: SmolLM2-135M under the section 2 pins, the section 13.1 prompt
+ids `6403, 1980, 253, 655`, `seed = 1`, `temperature = 0.8`, `top_k = 40`,
+`top_p = 0.95`.
+
+- 16 tokens: ids `28, 253, 555, 3365, 15688, 28203, 761, 354, 2359, 288,
+  724, 468, 511, 30, 909, 2275`;
+  `CIS2_REF = 98ed8bf7997816f366b7434f3fb097944cefe569debc75033f28390478f2aa35`;
+  sha256 of the 16 ids as u32 LE = `f8e7a6b6a0a6f54848d36f7fbaa08d3fcd2c89f49f5cfd585b6187c32985cfb7`.
+- 64 tokens:
+  `CIS2_REF = 6a760d9e4d7125517d8120c031327202ab1cffdb19edf24fc82b437884ccf55a`.
+
+Which builds reproduced these values is recorded in
+`docs/REPRODUCTIONS.md` (seeded-sampling rows). The greedy 16-token decode
+of every such build equals the pinned `d82743059d…` of section 13.1.
+
+## 14.7 What this does and does not claim
+
+The claim is bit-exact reproducibility of the drawn token sequence across
+implementations and hardware that implement this section. The statistical
+behaviour of the sampler is that of ordinary Gumbel-max sampling with
+temperature, top-k and top-p; nothing is claimed about sample quality. The
+hash is not a secret and is not required to be cryptographic.
